MICRO-INTERACTION 1: AIR PHYSICS (Springs & Wind)

The cards do not sit statically. They are bound by a 2D physics engine.

The Tethers (Spring Physics):

Every card has an invisible "anchor" point in the grid.

The card is attached to this anchor via a spring constraint (Hooke's Law).

If pushed, the card will swing back to its anchor with a damping effect
(friction: 0.85, tension: 0.05), eventually settling.

Visual Cue: A faint, 1px #CBD5E1 line connects the top center of the card
to an invisible point directly above it, mimicking a physical wire.

The Wind (Cursor Draft Repulsion):

The script calculates the velocity and trajectory of the user's cursor.

As the cursor sweeps past a card (radius < 200px), it applies a repulsive
force proportional to the mouse velocity.

The card moves away on the X/Y axes and slightly rotates (tilts) to
simulate aerodynamic drag.

MICRO-INTERACTION 2: GRAVITY WELLS (Black Holes)

The user can temporarily override the grid with gravity.

The Click (Spawn):

Clicking on any empty space (not a card, not the header) spawns a Gravity Well.

Visual: A small #0F172A dot appears with concentric #94A3B8 rings that
pulse outwards.

The Pull:

The well applies a constant attractive force to all Project Cards.

The cards stretch on their tethers, piling up around the gravity well.

The Reset (Header Click):

Clicking the [ MODERN GALLERY ] header instantly destroys the active
Gravity Well. The spring physics snap all cards back to their anchor points.

MICRO-INTERACTION 3: THE CURTAIN PULL (Project Details)

Instead of a standard click, viewing a project requires a tactile "drag" action.

The Affordance (Hover State):

When hovering over a specific card, the aerodynamic wind physics for that
card are paused.

The top-right corner of the card visually "folds" down slightly (using a
CSS clip-path or SVG overlay), revealing a darker layer underneath,
teaching the user it is a physical material.

The Drag (Active State):

The user clicks and drags the card to the left or right.

As they drag, the card visually stretches and "tears" open like a curtain
sliding on a rail.

The Reveal (Modal State):

Once dragged past a 30% threshold, the card snaps into a full-screen or
large-center modal.

The background darkens (background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.8)).

The detailed project blog (images, GitHub links, deep-dive text) is revealed.

The Project Tags ([Languages], [Tools], [AI]) detach from the card and
slowly orbit the opened modal like planets.

TECHNICAL UX & PERFORMANCE NOTES

Physics Implementation: Driving DOM elements with inline transform: translate(x, y) rotate(r)
via a requestAnimationFrame loop is highly performant for a small number
of elements (4-8 cards). You do NOT need Canvas for the cards themselves,
which ensures your text remains crisp and selectable for recruiters.

Draw the "tethers" and the "gravity well" rings on a single full-screen <canvas>
that sits strictly behind the DOM project cards using absolute positioning
and pointer-events: none.